    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). Done.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI added a city-capacity check to the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline, following the project's existing function-injection conventions (matching the sibling `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold`/`DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException` pattern).\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureCityCapacity.java` \u2014 reads the validated `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, counts existing owners in that city via `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is already \u2265 50. It runs *before* `build`/`save`, so the new owner isn't counted \u2014 meaning a 51st owner in a full city is rejected.\n- `CityAtCapacityException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it appears in the `throws` clause.\n- `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 escalation handler responding **409** with body `{\"errors\":[\"city\"]}`, mirroring the other owner 409 handlers.\n- `...owner.CityAtCapacityException.yml` \u2014 escalation wiring.\n\n**Edited:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `ensureCityCapacity` step (under `transaction` governance) between `ensureUniqueHousehold` and `build`.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to 409) passes, and the full suite reports `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\nNote: the per-city cap of 50 is currently a constant (`EnsureCityCapacity.CAPACITY`). If this limit ever needs to be configurable, that constant is the single place to change it.",
